    "public_summary": "Iran's internet remains in stable partial outage with all 4 major ISP international gateways unreachable from external probes while domestic and whitelisted foreign services operate normally. The selective-internet architecture is unchanged: 95.8% of IP ranges blocked, protocol whitelist enforced at 4.2% (45/1068), Electro DNS suppression continues, Snapp remains in redirect-loop state, Shaparak payment gateway timeout persists, Asiatech dual failure continues. A new development: TIC DNS (217.218.155.155) degraded dramatically from 223ms to 5,227ms, suggesting transient congestion or throttling on state-run DNS infrastructure. All circumvention tools (Tor, Snowflake, Psiphon) and foreign platforms remain fully accessible. Saman Bank latency improved slightly (3.1s to 2.9s).",
    "insight": "The TIC DNS 23x latency spike (223ms to 5,227ms) while Shecan DNS remains fast at 228ms is the only notable deviation in an otherwise identical pass — suggesting either transient internal congestion on TIC infrastructure or a brief throttling pulse on the state DNS resolver path, distinct from the persistent Electro DNS suppression targeting anti-censorship resolvers.",
    "recommendation": "Continue monitoring at current 10-minute intervals. Priority watch items: (1) TIC DNS — if elevated latency (5s+) persists for 3+ consecutive passes (30+ minutes), it signals sustained throttling of the state DNS resolver rather than transient congestion; (2) Snapp error state — redirect-loop persists across all observed passes, now well beyond the 60-minute threshold confirming persistent DPI rule-state issue rather than transient; (3) Saman Bank trending toward operational (2.9s) — watch for drop below 2,000ms threshold. No immediate action needed."
